---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import CountDemo from "../components/CountDemo.astro";
import { COUNT_BASE, SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";

const increment = `curl -X POST "${COUNT_BASE}/v1/counters/downloads?label=downloads" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY"
# {"counter":{"value":1,"badge_url":"${COUNT_BASE}/b/pub_…svg", …}}`;

const badge = `![downloads](${COUNT_BASE}/b/pub_….svg)`;

const read = `curl -s ${COUNT_BASE}/b/pub_….json
# {"name":"downloads","value":12300}`;

const sequence = `# create it once with the guarantee
curl -X POST "${COUNT_BASE}/v1/counters/invoice-2026?monotonic=true" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY"

# then just allocate. ?by=100 returns 102, so 3..102 are yours
curl -X POST "${COUNT_BASE}/v1/counters/invoice-2026?by=100" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY"

# and it will not go back
curl -X PUT "${COUNT_BASE}/v1/counters/invoice-2026?value=2" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY"   # 409`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`NanoCount — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="A named counter that cannot lose an increment. Read it as JSON, or as a small SVG badge you can paste anywhere. Free, one curl to start."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">nano-api / count</p>
					<h1>One number, and not worth<br /><span class="dim">a table of its own</span></h1>
					<p class="lede">
						Increments are a single SQL statement, so two callers at the same instant are never
						handed the same number. Read it back as JSON, or as an SVG badge that needs no token
						And because of that same property, it doubles as a sequence allocator.
					</p>
					<p class="cta">
						<a class="button" href="/howto/#setup">Take a key</a>
						<a class="button secondary" href="/howto/#count">Curl walkthrough</a>
					</p>
					<p class="fineprint muted">
						Free. Signup is one <code>curl</code> and the key comes back in the response. No form, no
						card, nobody to ask.
					</p>
				</div>
				<CountDemo />
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Increment from anywhere</h2>
				<pre><code>{increment}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					<code>?by=5</code> to add more, <code>PUT</code> to set an exact value. The counter is
					created on first use.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Reading it back</h2>
				<pre><code>{badge}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					<code>?label=</code> and <code>?color=</code> override it per request. Or read the raw
					number from a browser, since the JSON endpoint is CORS-open:
				</p>
				<pre><code>{read}</code></pre>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Invoice numbers are the same problem</h2>
				<p>
					This is the counter's best use, not a feature bolted onto it. Handing out invoice
					numbers means exactly what a counter already guarantees: two callers at the same
					instant must never get the same one. The atomic increment <em>is</em> the allocator,
					and <code>?by=100</code> reserves a block in one round trip rather than a hundred.
				</p>
				<p>
					One thing has to be forbidden. A tally may be corrected ("the download count is really
					12300" is reasonable), but invoice 42 twice is an accounting problem. Create it with
					<code>?monotonic=true</code> and the counter keeps everything it already did, minus the
					ability to move backwards: a negative step or a lower <code>PUT</code> gets a
					<code>409</code>.
				</p>
				<pre><code>{sequence}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					The flag is fixed at creation, because a counter that could drop the guarantee would not
					have one. It promises no allocation is handed out twice, not that the numbers have no
					gaps: a caller that dies after taking 43 loses 43. Where unbroken numbering is a legal
					requirement, that has to happen in the same transaction as the invoice, and no counter
					over HTTP can do that.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Three ways I got this wrong by hand</h2>
				<ol class="steps">
					<li>
						<span class="n">1</span>
						<h3>Two writers, one lost count</h3>
						<p>
							The obvious version reads the number, adds one, writes it back. Two callers who read
							the same moment write the same value, and an increment vanishes. Here adding and
							returning is one statement, so there is no gap to lose it in.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="n">2</span>
						<h3>The key ends up committed to git</h3>
						<p>
							A badge is an image, and an image URL cannot send a header, so it ends up in a
							README or a template with the key in it. Each counter gets its own unguessable
							public id instead: it shows that one number, cannot change it, and says nothing
							about the rest of your account.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="n">3</span>
						<h3>The number counts caches, not code</h3>
						<p>
							Put a CDN in front of a page-hit counter and it either freezes or inflates. Reading
							never increments here. You increment from your own code, when the thing you actually
							care about happened.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Using it with the others</h2>
				<p>
					Count the rows an export wrote, then ping <a href="/pulse/">Pulse</a> when it finishes.
					One number for the badge, one alert when the job stops.
				</p>
				<p class="cta">
					<a class="button" href="/howto/">How to, in curl</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href="/about/">What this is</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 1fr;
		gap: 2.5em;
		align-items: center;
		padding: 2em 0 1em;
	}
	.dim {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--muted);
		max-width: 34em;
	}
	.cta {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.7em;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0.6em;
	}
	.fineprint {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.band {
		padding: 3em 0 0;
	}
	.band > p {
		max-width: 46em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.small {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.steps {
		list-style: none;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
		gap: 1.2em;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0;
		padding: 0;
	}
	.steps li {
		border-top: 2px solid var(--border);
		padding-top: 1em;
	}
	.steps .n {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		color: var(--alive-ink);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
	}
	.steps h3 {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		margin: 0.2em 0 0.4em;
	}
	.steps p {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 0.93rem;
		margin: 0;
	}
	@media (max-width: 860px) {
		.hero,
		.steps {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
